<speak>
<p>We know how to shift a row. Now reverse the order in which the cyclic positions are read. If h of X has coefficients h zero through h n minus one, define h star by replacing X with X inverse. Since X inverse equals X to the n minus one, the result stays in the same group ring.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>The constant coefficient stays fixed. The remaining positions are read backward. Use four generic coefficients to see this clearly: a, b, c, d becomes a, d, c, b. The coefficients keep their values; only their cyclic positions reverse.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Return to our original polynomial, negative one plus X plus X squared plus X cubed. Its star is negative one plus X inverse plus X to the negative two plus X to the negative three. Reduce exponents modulo four. The result is negative one plus X cubed plus X squared plus X, which is our original polynomial. This particular row is unchanged because its final three coefficients are equal.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Why is star well defined? The rule defining our group ring is X to the n equals one. Its inverse also has nth power one. Replacing X by X inverse therefore respects that defining relation, so equal ring elements still have equal stars.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Reverse twice, and every position returns to where it started. The star of h star is h. Replacing X by X inverse also respects multiplication. In this commutative group ring, the star of f times g is f star times g star. Thus star is an involution of the ring.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Remember what star does: it reverses cyclic positions. It neither negates the coefficients nor takes the reciprocal of the polynomial. We introduced it because multiplying h by its reversal will encode every cyclic dot product in a single polynomial.<break time="900ms"/></p>
</speak>
